WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.581
Why RAG exists, and why it fails quietly.

2
00:00:02.861 --> 00:00:08.664
The RAG paper (Lewis and colleagues, 2020) framed the problem this studio solves.

3
00:00:08.944 --> 00:00:20.272
Language models store a lot of knowledge in their parameters, but they can’t easily show where an answer came from or update what they know, and the authors described both as open research problems.

4
00:00:20.552 --> 00:00:31.176
Their proposal was a second, non-parametric memory: an external index (Wikipedia, in their case) that a retriever reads from and that you can update without retraining.

5
00:00:31.456 --> 00:00:34.187
That is what your vector and keyword indexes are.

6
00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:39.565
The catch is that RAG adds stages, and every stage can fail without raising an error.

7
00:00:39.845 --> 00:00:42.043
A missed passage doesn’t throw an exception.

8
00:00:42.323 --> 00:00:44.328
A truncated context doesn’t crash.

9
00:00:44.608 --> 00:00:51.093
The model writes a fluent answer from whatever it got, and the result looks the same whether the pipeline worked or not.

10
00:00:51.373 --> 00:01:00.483
This is why the checks from lesson 10 matter, and why this lesson’s main skill is diagnosis: given a wrong answer, find the stage that lost the truth.
